The Problem: The "If/Else" State Machine

Imagine you are building a Document Publishing System (like WordPress). An Article can be in three states: DRAFT,
REVIEW, or PUBLISHED.

The Beginner Way:
The junior developer uses an enum and writes massive switch statements inside every single method of the Document class.

Why a Senior rejects this: What happens when the boss adds a new state tomorrow called "ARCHIVED"? You have to open the
Document class, find every single method, and add another else if to all of them. The file becomes 2,000 lines long and
incredibly fragile.
The Senior Solution: The State Pattern

The State Pattern says: "Take the behaviors of each state and extract them into their own separate classes!"

Instead of the Document class tracking a String and using if statements, it holds a State object. When the user clicks
"Publish", the Document just blindly hands the command to the current State object. When the state changes, the object
swaps itself out for a new one!